.TH GEMTEXT2PDF 1 "2026-11-09"
.SH NAME
gemtext2pdf \- convert Gemtext to PDF
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gemtext2pdf
.RB [ \-o\  \fIFILENAME\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-style\  \fISTYLE\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-italics ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gemtext2pdf
reads Gemtext on standard input and writes a PDF to standard output, or to
the file named with
.BR \-o .
The PDF is produced by converting the input to
.BR groff_mom (7)
as
.BR gemtext2mom (1)
does, and running the result through
.BR "groff \-mom \-Tpdf" ,
so
.BR groff (1)
must be on the
.BR PATH .
Links in the input become clickable links in the PDF.
The title of the document is the text of its first heading.
If the document begins with that heading, it appears only as the title;
otherwise it appears as the title and also remains in the text where it
was.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o\  FILENAME
Write the PDF to
.I FILENAME
instead of standard output.
If conversion fails, the file is removed.
.TP
.BI \-\-style\  STYLE
Select the visual style.
.I STYLE
is one of
.BR default ,
.BR sans\-serif
and
.BR cap\-heads ;
the default is
.BR default .
See
.BR STYLES .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-italics
Leave asterisks as they are instead of setting the text between them in
italics.
See
.BR ITALICS .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message and exit.
.SH INPUT
The input format is described in
.BR gemtext (7).
.SH STYLES
The following styles are available.
.TP
.B default
Serif text (Times), bold headings, no paragraph indent, slightly less
than a line of space between paragraphs, and indented bulleted lists.
.TP
.B sans-serif
As
.BR default ,
but in a sans-serif font (Helvetica).
.TP
.B cap-heads
As
.BR default ,
but the title and the headings are set in small capitals of normal weight
rather than in bold.
.PP
In every style, running page headers are omitted and page numbers are
printed without surrounding dashes.
.SH ITALICS
Unless
.B \-\-no\-italics
is given, a span of text enclosed in asterisks, such as
.BR *like\ this* ,
is set in italics, with the asterisks removed.
The rules, which apply to each paragraph, list item and quotation
separately, are these:
.IP \(bu 3
An opening asterisk must be at the start of the text, or follow whitespace
or one of the characters
.BR ( ,
.BR [ ,
.BR { ,
.B \(dq
or
.BR \(aq .
It must be followed by a character that is neither whitespace nor an
asterisk.
.IP \(bu 3
A closing asterisk must be preceded by a character that is neither
whitespace nor an asterisk.
It must be at the end of the text, or be followed by whitespace or one of the
characters
.BR . ,
.BR , ,
.BR ; ,
.BR : ,
.BR ! ,
.BR ? ,
.BR ) ,
.BR ] ,
.BR } ,
.B \(dq
or
.BR \(aq .
.IP \(bu 3
Each opening asterisk is paired with the nearest following closing
asterisk.
The span must contain at least one character.
.IP \(bu 3
Spans do not nest and do not extend beyond one paragraph, list item or
quotation line.
.IP \(bu 3
Any asterisk that is not part of a pair is left as it is, so
.B 2 * 3 * 4
and
.B a*b*c
are unaffected.
.IP \(bu 3
A doubled asterisk, as in
.BR **text** ,
is never treated as italics.
.IP \(bu 3
Underscores have no special meaning.
.IP \(bu 3
Preformatted blocks, headings, link addresses and link labels are not
searched for italics.
.PP
There is no way to escape an asterisk.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
gemtext2pdf \-o page.pdf < page.gmi
gemtext2pdf \-\-style cap\-heads < page.gmi | lpr
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success, and non-zero if the input is malformed, or if a write or other
error occurs.
A diagnostic is printed to standard error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gemtext (7),
.BR gemtext2md (1),
.BR gemtext2mom (1),
.BR groff (1),
.BR groff_mom (7)
.SH AUTHOR
Martin Keegan
